    "q-74365": {
      "dom_id": "step-q74365",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "For the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{x},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $y\\to 0$ as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n\nII. The domain of the function is $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$\n\nIII. The function is a decreasing function.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=-\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14230521/assets/e3f5ed500c81d09e2775f09fd10a221d7ec0fca7e39249b7add7a680328b68c1.png)\n\nNow, we analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $y\\to 0$ as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is defined on all real numbers except $0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ is an increasing function.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
